  "id": "DrugBank_DB08806 - MONDO_2177",
  "verbalization": "Roxatidine Acetate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hyperinsulinism (disease) through a mechanism involving gastrin secretion abnormalities. The evidence suggests that Roxatidine Acetate may modulate gastric acid secretion, as it is associated with Gastrin Secretion Abnormality, which in turn is linked to Dexrabeprazole and Cimetidine, both of which are known to influence gastric function. These connections imply that Roxatidine Acetate could potentially affect gastric acid secretion, which is a key factor in the development of Hyperinsulinism. The presence of multiple indirect pathways—through Gastrin Secretion Abnormality and gastric ulceration—suggests a possible role in the regulation of gastrointestinal function, which may influence insulin secretion. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Roxatidine Acetate to contribute to the pathophysiology of Hyperinsulinism through its effects on gastric acid secretion and related processes.",
